course: ai
lesson: 11
title: School rules, honesty and wrong accusations
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sam takes a bot's story, changes three words and calls it his own. That is still copying, the way repainting your mate's bike doesn't make it your bike.
explain: On Lily's "no screens" writing day, the class writes by hand, and her story is wild and funny because it is hers. Writing on your own builds your writing muscles.
explain: There are honest ways to use a bot: practice questions for a test, or "How can I make this better?" about an essay you wrote, then changing bits yourself. Mia's teacher says, "If you used AI, tell me how. Honesty gets credit."
explain: Dishonest ways include traced bot drawings, copied reading-log comments that all sound the same, and selling "AI homework" for sweets at break.
explain: Some skills, like spelling "necessary", need to live in your own head, and sometimes helping someone, like a little brother, means not helping him cheat.
fact: Changing a few words in someone else's writing does not make it yours; it is still copying.
fact: Writing without help shows what you can really do and makes you a better writer.
fact: An art teacher wants to see your own drawing, wobbly lines and all, not a traced copy.
fact: Asking a bot for practice questions is a smart way to revise, if they cover the right topics.
fact: Reading-log comments are meant to show what you thought, so copied ones are pointless.
fact: Getting feedback on your own work, then improving it yourself, is a good use if your school allows it.
fact: Being open about how you used a tool is honest, and many teachers give credit for it.
fact: Helping others cheat, or selling them copied work, is wrong too.
fact: Some skills, like spelling, need to be practised in your own head without a checker, because you will need them when no tool is around.
fact: "Necessary" is spelled with one c and a double s.
fact: Telling a grown-up kindly about a little brother's copied homework helps him learn.
exercise: Sam changes three words in a bot's story and calls it his. Is it his? | No, changing a few words is still copying | Yes, any change at all makes it his own story | Yes, if he changes the title too | No, unless he changes exactly five words
exercise: Lily's handwritten story on "no screens" day is wild and funny. What does that show? | Writing on her own shows what she can really do | Handwriting is always funnier than typing, for everyone | Screens make stories boring by law | The teacher secretly wrote it for her
exercise: Connor traces the bot's drawing for art homework. What did the teacher want? | His own drawing, wobbly lines and all | A perfect drawing from anywhere he can find one | A drawing traced from one by a famous artist | A photo from the internet instead of a drawing
exercise: Chloe uses the bot for practice questions and practises them. Is that a good idea? | Yes, if she checks the topics with her teacher | No, practising for tests is cheating | Yes, and she should copy the answers into the test | No, bots can't write questions
exercise: Maddie's mum notices her bot-written reading-log comments all sound the same. What would be better? | One honest sentence she wrote herself | A longer bot comment with more long words in it | A comment from a friend | No comment at all
exercise: Jake writes his own essay, then asks the bot how to improve it, changing bits himself. Is that OK? | Yes, if allowed: feedback is fine and his words stay his | No, he must never show anyone his work, not even a teacher | Yes, and the bot should rewrite all of it | No, essays must never be improved
exercise: Mia's teacher says "If you used AI, tell me how." Why does that matter? | Being open about tools is honest | Teachers want to buy the same bot for themselves | It makes the work look much longer | It lets the teacher blame the bot
exercise: Sam's friend sells "AI homework" for sweets. What should Sam do? | Say no and don't join in | Buy some, sweets are cheap | Start selling it at school too | Tell everyone it's perfectly fine
exercise: Lily spells "necessary" in the spelling bee with no bots. Which spelling is right? | necessary | neccessary | necesary | neccesary
exercise: Connor sees a bot wrote his little brother's reading homework. What is kind and right? | Tell Mum kindly and help him do it properly | Laugh and do nothing, it's not his business | Do all his homework for him | Tell the whole class
exercise: Why does copying your reading-log comments make them pointless? | They're meant to show what you thought | Teachers never read them, so it doesn't matter | The bot thinks for you anyway | Comments must be copied by rule
exercise: Why should some skills live in your own head? | You will need them when no tools are around | Bots are banned in every building in the country | Your head has more memory than a bot | Tools never work on Tuesdays
exercise: What turns bot practice questions into good revision? | Answering them yourself and checking topics | Reading the bot's answers only | Asking for easier questions | Doing them during the real test
exercise: Which is honest use of AI at school, when allowed? | Getting feedback, then improving your own words | Changing three words in a bot story | Tracing a bot's drawing | Selling bot homework
test: "i changed like half the words so it's mine now" Is it? | No, it's still copying someone else's work | Yes, half the words is more than enough to make it yours | Yes, as long as the title is new | Only if no one finds out
test: What would happen if everyone traced bot drawings for art? | Nobody would learn to draw | Everyone would become great artists | The art teacher would be thrilled | The bot would win the art prize
test: A cartoon kid sells "homework by robot, 2 sweets each" and becomes a hero. What's wrong? | Helping others cheat is wrong, not heroic | Two sweets is too cheap | Robots don't do homework | Heroes always sell things
test: Maddie wants her reading-log done quickly. What's the honest shortcut? | Write one true sentence herself | Copy a bot's comment and change the name | Copy out last week's comment again | Ask her big sister to write it for her
test: "if i tell the teacher i used AI i'll get in trouble" What did Mia's teacher say? | Honesty about tools gets credit | You'll get a zero just for being honest | Never tell, teachers don't really care | Only tell the teacher if you get caught
test: Connor's brother is 6 and used a bot for reading homework. Why does Connor tell Mum? | So his brother actually learns to read well | To get his little brother punished by Mum | Because he wants the bot himself | Because Mum asked him to spy on his brother
test: Your friend says spelling bees are pointless because "phones fix spelling". Why still learn? | Some skills need to live in your own head | Phones never fix spelling | Spelling bees win money | Teachers like long words
test: Jake's bot says "rewrite this paragraph like this:" and gives a whole new paragraph. What keeps it honest? | Taking the idea but writing it in his own words | Pasting the bot's paragraph in where his used to be | Asking the bot to make it longer | Deleting his own paragraph
test: Chloe's practice questions are about volcanoes, but the test is on rivers. What did she forget? | To check the test topics with her teacher | To ask the bot for harder volcano questions | To print the questions | To learn about lava
test: Which pupil was most honest? | Mia, who said exactly how she used a bot | Sam, who changed three words | Connor, who traced a drawing | Maddie, whose comments all sounded the same
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of a small number of words in text produced by a chatbot? | It does not render the text the original work of the person who altered it | It confers full authorship upon the person who altered it | It is permissible provided the title is likewise altered | It renders the text the property of the school
exam: It is given that a pupil discloses to the teacher the manner in which a chatbot was employed. Identify the principal merit of such disclosure. | It constitutes honest conduct, for which credit may be given | It transfers responsibility for the work to the chatbot | It obliges the teacher to award full marks | It exempts the pupil from all further assessment
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the sale of work produced by a chatbot to other pupils. | It constitutes assistance in cheating and is wrong | It is an acceptable form of commerce in schools | It is permissible if the price is modest | It is wrong only if the work contains errors of fact
exam: Ascertain the correct orthography of the word signifying "required". | necessary | neccessary | necesary | neccesary
